{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1347631-has-anyone-run-a-neolithic-chronicle-yet","id":1347631,"title":"Has anyone run a Neolithic chronicle yet?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-11-11T00:43:24-08:00","id":1347631,"title":"Has anyone run a Neolithic chronicle yet?","content":"Would love to hear some actual stories.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-11T03:10:29-08:00","id":1347648,"title":"","content":"Did not run it - but is extensive playing <b>Far Cry: Primal,</b> lately, so have some ideas or advice, if anyone want's it. It's in Mesolithic era, 10000 BCE, so it can have some difference than <i>Sundered World</i> Dark Era, that is 5500 BCE - but I could hash differences.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LJ2iH57Fs3M\" data-code=\"LJ2iH57Fs3M\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/LJ2iH57Fs3M/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-11T11:35:25-08:00","id":1347724,"title":"","content":"Only for a story where modern characters went back in time. It was Mage, but no one had Space and when they got split up in the beginning they screwed up big by not following any real logic when it came to reuniting. So that took a while to deal with while two settled in with a settlement (they had to work to help out as Wise) and the others sort of went the right direction by mistake (they were following signs of the General, which pointed to the East).<br /> <br /> Lets see. Big plots at the time, a werewolf had encountered an idigam and, unable to deal with it on their own, stalked and kidnapped one of the PCs to help them out. A small settlement had been usurped by a strix and turned into vampiric minions and were raiding nearby settlements for blood dolls (and setting out traps to catch other strix that may have escaped). A Father-inspired Noah was also gathering people for his proto-Exarchic cult. And the influences of the General were encouraging all of it while its spirits and minions were heading to kill the Pangaean Ant, who was setting up an angle to resurrect herself in the future (using the PCs). She had a legacy of mages, the Drones, who gave up much of their individuality to serve her.<br /> <br /> EDIT: The players really liked it. You lose a lot, getting rid of the modern nights angle. Been wanting to do a proper one for Werewolf but haven't gotten around to it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 11-11-2019, 02:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-11T19:00:51-08:00","id":1347815,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1347631-has-anyone-run-a-neolithic-chronicle-yet?p=1347648#post1347648\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Did not run it - but is extensive playing <b>Far Cry: Primal,</b> lately, so have some ideas or advice, if anyone want's it. It's in Mesolithic era, 10000 BCE, so it can have some difference than <i>Sundered World</i> Dark Era, that is 5500 BCE - but I could hash differences.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LJ2iH57Fs3M\" data-code=\"LJ2iH57Fs3M\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/LJ2iH57Fs3M/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>I also enjoyed Primal, but there\u2019s a world of difference between the two that makes it not terribly helpful, IMHO. The folk in Primal are still wowed by fire; the Vinca were forging things out of copper.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-14T09:53:38-08:00","id":1348498,"title":"","content":"Our group has run three, but none of them were long-running and none quite did the setting justice.<br /> <br /> I think a lot of that has to do with the fact that, when playing historical eras, we tend to get caught up on details a lot; stuff like 'would the doors be wooden or cloth' and 'were domesticated animals a thing yet'. A lot of prep and research went into every game but a few questions always came up that ground the game to a halt due to our general (unhealthy) refusal as a group to let any details like this ride.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-14T10:02:22-08:00","id":1348501,"title":"","content":"Though I unfortunately don't have any stories to tell about this I would say that the idea is brilliant and that I am always on the look out for options, ideas, and rules for doing such a thing.<br /> <br /> Of course the question becomes do you go the route that the people are fully of that time and thus their worldview is only of that time or do you go the route of somehow having people from the modern day being transported to the past. If you go that way then you have the ability to have modern sensibilities and thought processes come into play, but also the element that the players might have their characters try and develop advanced technology. Both ways I think would be fun and would enable the telling of a fantastic story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/661-lordheru","name":"LordHeru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar661_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-15T11:20:35-08:00","id":1348695,"title":"","content":"I wrote a one-shot adventure for Neolithic Mage that I\u2019ve run a few times for different groups. It\u2019s one of my favorites to run, actually. I do use a dice/system hack for it, which has helped jump people in quicker - but it does use the FFG dice (edge of the empire / genesys), so every action tends to have a lot addition setbacks, bonus, exceptionals, or dramatic failures. But the setting and story is purely set in the Sundered World.<br /> <br /> <br /> Its a pretty classic adventure story overall. The local wise gather for the most sacred ritual of the year, and notice a ping of magic that could threaten everything. With a little divination, it\u2019s revealed that the Pangaean of the Sun has vanished (specifically, the Nourishing Sun) - taking all the actual sun\u2019s affect on the world (like heat, growing crops..) and they travel across the primeval wilderness to find and rescue the sun. With a nice twist that this Pangaean was in fact imprisoned by Atlanteans in the Time Before, but their temple projected its power across the world - so it was \u201cfine\u201d until the temple broke. So they have to fight a spirit of Hunger who broke the temple to start with, and escort the Sun out of the Death Swamp to where it can ascend back to the sky. And as a reward, they\u2019re given an Omphalos stone, told to keep its fire burning forever, and asked to create a cult devoted to the sun.<br /> I always tell an epilogue, to show its consequences spreading out to the future, where the flame has been tended six thousand years and, when destroyed by invaders, all of Europe suffers a century long famine just before getting hit by the Black Plague. Which is true ... the eternal flame being broken in ~1200 AD is probably a coincidence, but it makes for really good fiction.<br /> <br /> Very mythic, very classic fantasy adventure. I decided to write that type of story mostly to make it easier to jump people in as a one shot, but also because I really wanted practice for wilderness adventures (choosing routes, complications with rations, random encounters) - and then a whole heaping of \u201cPrincess Mononoke is awesome\u201d.<br /> And I based the setting and pangaeans on Lithuanian mythology - which I learned for another game, and pretty well fell in love with.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> For some specific stories that have come up from running this:<ul><li>They\u2019ve come across an Atlantean shrine in the mountains, holding an artifact that controlled the weather. Due almost entirely to about a dozen rolls of only exceptional success or dramatic failure, a Pangaean of storms coveted it, dragged them into the Border Marches, and they finally negotiated aide on their quest for it to keep the artifact.</li> <li>They\u2019ve come across a village people tried to settle without a Wise to protect them, all of whom became spirit claimed, and hunted by a werewolf pack - a pack they had to parlay with to pass through.</li> <li>They\u2019ve recruited a Wolf Blooded outlander from the Cult of the Hunter to accompany them.</li> <li>They came across a dead lands of Atlantean design, where ghosts were trapped in statues, presumably as some kind of border security or maybe from the place being ground down by dissonance. My players decided to actually <i>explore </i>that place, which wore them down a lot, but earned them an Artifact of death magic.</li> <li>They\u2019ve passed through a swamp village, far from their home valley, devoted to the pangaean Owl (unclean death), who had Proto-Vampires protecting them in place of Wise.</li> <li>And they met a bunny spirit who, with plants no longer gaining nutrients since the loss of the sun, pleaded with them to give it a knife so it could teach it\u2019s warren to hunt and eat meat instead. That was fun and unsettling; they took that as a nice kick to finish this quest before the natural order fell entirely to pieces.</li> </ul><br /> \u200bI\u2019ve used a lot of fun descriptions running this, particularly with Atlantean shrines, which had anachronistic technology like glass and thauminum manacles, and in the Border Marches - they\u2019ve been to both a mountain range, and to Owl\u2019s personal swamp of death. The intro scene is pretty well always fun, with four villages gathered together so that their Wise can perform the midsummer ritual - both religious and magical - and the role play that has happened from players having to break the news that they need to go on a quest, leaving everyone defenseless for who knows how long - but without causing mass hysteria.<br /> <br /> <br /> Sundered world is more or less my favorite setting ever. I\u2019ve had a long lasting love of historical fantasy - and I\u2019m pretty much a history / mythology / evolution nerd, so this hits so many of my passions (and things I\u2019ve actually studied!). I would love to run a longer chronicle of this, or even write some more one shots, but my ST hands are full right now. I\u2019d probably love being a player in Sundered World more.... or even it being a video game. It has some majestic descriptions that I\u2019d love to visually see, and it begs for exploration - which is fun as a ST, but more so as a PC.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data